<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Blue Bus.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Draw a single picture that represents a simple story or event. Communication: Lead a simple classroom activity like a lineup or cleanup using gestures and basic words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use gestures or drawings to describe transportation vehicles in the classroom (e.g., drawing a picture of a car or miming the act of driving a car to describe the word "car"). Can clarify responses by pointing to pictures of transportation vehicles when needed. Can use basic words and body language to explain how transportation vehicles are used during classroom activities (e.g., pretending to drive by moving hands like holding a steering wheel).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 47%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #e0e0e0; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Vehicle Action" TPR game. Teacher calls out "Green car, go!" and students pretend to drive a green car. "Red bus, stop!" and students pretend to stop a red bus. "Yellow bike, ride!" and students pretend to ride a yellow bike. This activates prior knowledge of vehicles, colors, and actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models drawing a simple vehicle and describing its color and name. For example, the teacher draws a bus on the board, colors it blue, and says, "This is a blue bus. It is a blue bus." The teacher uses clear gestures for drawing and coloring. Then, the teacher demonstrates miming driving a car while saying, "I drive a car."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple yes/no questions and "What" questions, encouraging gestures or pointing.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a drawing of a red car) "Is this a green car?" (Students shake heads for no or nod for yes if it is green).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a blue bus) "What color is the bus?" (Students may say "Blue!" or point to the color blue).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Miming driving) "Am I driving a bike?" (Students shake heads for no).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors of Things that Go"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content:</b> The teacher uses a set of large, colorful flashcards or a big picture book featuring various transportation vehicles in different colors (e.g., a green car, a blue bus, a red bike, a yellow truck, a purple train, an orange taxi). The teacher shows each card, names the vehicle and its color, and asks students to repeat chorally and individually, emphasizing pronunciation /k/ in "car" and /b/ in "bus" and /y/ in "yellow". The teacher also mimes the action for each vehicle (e.g., pretending to drive a car, ride a bike, go on a train).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards or a big picture book with illustrations of: car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi, in colors: green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange. The material presents simple, clear images of each vehicle with its corresponding color.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing objects: When describing colors in transportation, use phrases that describe vehicles, like "It is a red bike." "That is a green car." or "It's a blue bus." Answering questions about colors and objects: Respond with one-word answers or gestures for vehicles and colors (e.g., "Point to the blue bus.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher asks simple questions and uses gestures.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "What color is this bus?" (showing a blue bus card). Students point to the blue bus.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Is this a red bike?" (showing a red bike card). Students nod for yes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Please, point to the yellow taxi." Students point to the correct flashcard.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using drawings and gestures to clarify and describe transportation vehicles and their colors. Grammar: Color + noun (e.g., "green car"), WH-questions ("What color is the car?"), Present simple ("It is a red bike."). Vocabulary: car, bus, bike, truck, train, taxi; green, blue, red, yellow, purple, orange; drive, ride, see, go, stop, be; this, that; on.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Draw and Color Command:</b> The teacher provides students with outline drawings of different vehicles and crayons. The teacher gives commands like, "Color the car red," or "Color the bus blue." Students follow the instruction by coloring their drawings. Afterward, the teacher points to a student's colored drawing and asks, "What is this?" or "What color is this car?" Students respond with gestures or simple words like "Red!" or "It's a red car." This activity focuses on mediating instructions into a visual representation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Vehicle Charades and Guess:</b> The teacher says a vehicle name and color, e.g., "A green car." Students then either draw a green car or mime driving a car. The teacher asks a student, "What is he/she doing?" or "What color is the car?" The student who is miming or drawing might point to the vehicle they are representing or use a gesture. This practice reinforces using body language or visual cues to describe.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Picture Clarification Pairs:</b> Students work in pairs. Each student has 3-4 simple drawings of colored vehicles (e.g., a red bike, a blue truck). Student A describes one of their drawings without showing it, for example, "It's a red bike." Student B listens and tries to draw a red bike based on the description, or points to their own drawing that matches. Then they compare drawings. If there's a misunderstanding, they use gestures or point to their original drawing to clarify. The teacher circulates, encouraging polite requests like "Please, point to the blue car."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors of Things that Go"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> Students will present their "Vehicle Color Matching" game pieces, using their drawings, basic words, and gestures to explain how they match vehicle drawings to color labels. They will mediate the concept of "color + vehicle" through their creations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group demonstration and explanation of the "Vehicle Color Matching" game. Students will showcase their project by explaining a match to peers or the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students, in small groups (2-3), receive their prepared drawings of different colored vehicles (e.g., a green car, a red bus, a yellow taxi) and separate small cards with corresponding color labels (e.g., a green square, a red square, a yellow square).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Each student in the group takes turns holding up one of their vehicle drawings and its matching color label.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Student A holds up a drawing of a blue bus and the blue color label. Student A says, "This is a blue bus," and points clearly to the drawing and then to the color label, showing the match. They can also mime driving a bus.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Other group members or the teacher can ask, "What color is the car, please?" or "Is this a green bus?" (pointing to a different vehicle). The presenting student uses their drawing and pointing gestures to respond and clarify their match.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. The activity focuses on using visual aids (their drawings) and gestures to explain and describe, mediating the information to others. For example, a student might pretend to hold a steering wheel while presenting a 'red car' drawing.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Vehicle Color Matching" presentations. The teacher observes individual students' ability to mediate information using their drawings and simple language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student selects two of their "Vehicle Color Matching" pairs (e.g., a red car drawing with a red color label, a blue bus drawing with a blue color label). They hold them up and use gestures and basic words to describe the match to the teacher or a peer. (e.g., "Red car!" while pointing to both). They can also mime how the vehicle moves.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students attempt to pronounce vehicle names and colors (e.g., /k/ in "car", /b/ in "bus", /y/ in "yellow").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least two color + vehicle combinations (e.g., "red car," "blue bus").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity through gestures/drawings:</b> Students effectively use pointing, gestures, or their drawings to explain the color-vehicle match.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students participate in group discussions, helping peers find matches or clarifying with gestures.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple drawing of three faces (happy, neutral, sad).
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students draw a circle around the face that shows how well they can match colors to vehicles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, they point to their favorite vehicle drawing from their project and show it to a friend or the teacher.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: bold; color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What new colorful vehicle did you draw today? (Students can point to their drawing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can you show me a blue bus with your drawing or by miming?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Vehicle Color Matching" project successfully allowed students to use drawings and simple language to mediate information. The use of gestures and pointing was crucial for clarity at this level. Students responded well to the hands-on drawing and matching activities. Some students still need more prompting for verbal output, relying heavily on gestures. For the next lesson in a new theme, I will ensure continued emphasis on multi-sensory learning and offer sentence frames for those who need more support in verbalizing their descriptions, even as we introduce new vocabulary. The pronunciation practice of initial sounds was effective for some, but others need more targeted drills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "Colors of Things that Go." The next lesson will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, potentially focusing on "My Family" or "Animals." The mediation skills practiced today, such as using visuals and gestures to explain, will be transferable to describing new concepts.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>